  dailyRead: "The most useful thing on this page today is that three triggers written down in advance were all tested inside twenty-four hours, and two of them moved the board. Start with the one that did not. Yesterday this file wrote that what takes USD off +3 is a soft Philly Fed combined with claims above 207K - the first crack in the growth leg the September projections leaned on. Both printed and both went the other way: the Philadelphia Fed index came in at 37.8 against a 30.5 consensus, down from 47.4 but comfortably above expectations, and initial jobless claims FELL to 196,000 against a 208,000 consensus from 206,000, with continuing claims down to 1.730 million from 1.769 million and the four-week average at 203,000. A labour market printing sub-200K claims is not one that forces a pause, and the dollar holds +3 with its stated downside test explicitly failed. The price is consolidating rather than extending, which is the appropriate response to a made move: DXY is 100.249 and +0.03% on the day, with the week +1.14% and the month +1.43%. The second trigger cut the euro. Yesterday EUR was held at +1 on an explicit two-sided argument - a soft Q2 wage print at 3.0% from 3.4% against the first sustained fall in European gas in a month - and it was stated that those two offset. Both halves moved the same way overnight. Eurostat's final August HICP revised the headline DOWN to 3.2% from the 3.3% flash, core eased to 2.4% from 2.5%, and the number that matters most fell hardest: SERVICES inflation dropped to 3.0% from 3.3% in July. Services is the ECB's domestic-inflation gauge and wages are its second-round gauge, and both are now cooling within eight days of a hike that was justified by upside inflation risks and a 2027 forecast revised up to 2.5%. What is left holding the 3.2% up is energy at +14.3% y/y, the highest since January 2023 - which is to say the euro area's inflation is an import bill, not a domestic overheat, and an import bill is precisely what a central bank looks through. Meanwhile the offsetting leg has already half-retraced: EU gas is 77.49 and UP 1.49% on the day with the weekly fall shrinking to -2.55% from -5.20%, UK gas is 194.56 and UP 2.01% with its week at -1.87% from -6.08%. One day of relief, then a bounce. Cut EUR to 0. The third trigger raised Australia, and it is the most forward-looking item on the page. Governor Bullock told the House of Representatives Standing Committee on Economics that the upside inflation risks the RBA flagged in August are NOW MATERIALISING, naming Middle East oil, the AI investment boom and extreme weather, while acknowledging that growth is slowing and unemployment is holding at 4.5%. Yesterday this file described the September 29 debate as inflation-too-high-with-excess-demand against policy-already-restrictive-with-unemployment-rising, and observed that the data had been drifting toward the second camp. The governor has just publicly planted herself in the first. That does not make a hike likely - TE still carries 4.35% for September 29 - but it changes the distribution of the statement and it lands on a currency already outperforming: AUD/USD is 0.71284 and +0.25% on a firm dollar, the month is POSITIVE at +0.05% and the year-to-date +6.83%, the only major on this board with a positive month. Copper corroborates, with its monthly figure flipping positive to +1.12% at 6.5602. AUD goes to +2. Fourth, the yen, and this is a cut made with the press conference still ahead, so the reasoning has to be explicit. The BoJ raised the policy rate to 1.25% from 1.00% at 03:00Z, TE-verified against a 1.25% consensus and TE's own 1.25% house forecast, taking the rate to its highest since the mid-1990s and shortening the gap between hikes to three months from six. Yesterday this page said plainly that the vote split was not published in any reachable source and was not assumed. It is now published, and it is 7-2 with Toichiro Asada and Ayano Sato dissenting AGAINST the increase. That is new information and it points the dovish way: a board that carried its July hold 8-1 with one hawkish dissent now carries a hike with two dovish ones, which caps the pace regardless of what the statement says about upside risk to the 2% target. Set it beside August core CPI at 1.7%, below the 1.8% consensus and down from 1.8%, and the sell-side path - a Reuters poll carrying 1.5% by March 2027 and 1.75% by Q2 - looks further from TE's model, which holds 1.25% at October 30 and trends back toward 1.00% in 2027. The tape has already voted: USD/JPY is 157.120 and +0.74% on the day, +2.28% on the week, a seventh consecutive session of yen underperformance, with EUR/JPY 180.519, GBP/JPY 210.090, AUD/JPY 112.014 and CHF/JPY 190.892 all up between 0.72% and 1.00%. Holding a +1 on the worst-performing currency of the week, whose only remaining support is an untested intervention threat, would be carrying a stale call; JPY goes to 0, not -1, because 1.25% is genuine tightening and 157 is deeper into the zone where the Ministry of Finance bought yen twelve days ago at a better level. The practical consequence is that the long-yen tickets this file has run all week - GBP/JPY, CHF/JPY and CAD/JPY short - come off to RANGE, AUD/JPY goes LONG, and JPYBASKET goes SHORT. They were wrong this week and the cleanest thing to do is say so and cut them. Fifth, oil and the metals, which are now one trade. Crude fell a third consecutive session to 100.963, -0.93%, with Brent 103.602 and -1.16%, and the reason is a genuine change in the supply picture rather than sentiment: Saudi Arabia is rerouting crude THROUGH the Strait of Hormuz on shuttle vessels, ship-to-ship transfers near Oman's Sohar port have risen to 2.7 million bpd from 1.5 million in August, and the kingdom has guided that roughly half the damaged East-West pipeline capacity returns within days with full operations inside six weeks. That falling energy premium pulled inflation expectations and yields down with it, and the metals took the gift - gold rose about 2% to 4367.03 with its WEEKLY figure turning positive at +0.38% from -0.45%, and silver is 66.127, +1.43% on the day and +2.90% on the week, the strongest move on the commodity board. Yesterday this file said a market that gets its bearish catalyst and refuses to break is not one to re-short; it has now done better than refuse to break, so XAU and XAG come off RANGE to LONG, with silver carrying the stronger case on a sixth consecutive annual supply deficit and lease rates above 5%. One caution that must be kept on the page: the weekly figure for WTI has turned POSITIVE at +0.91%, and yesterday's stated restore-CAD-to-plus-one trigger named exactly that. It should not be applied. The weekly only turned positive because the seven-day comparison rolled past the September 10-11 pipeline-strike spike; the spot price has FALLEN from 102.173 to 100.963 and has now dropped three sessions running. The substance of the trigger - oil paying Canada again - is not met, so CAD stays at 0. The cleanest expressions today are the Australian longs against the two weakest legs, AUD/NZD and AUD/CAD, the kiwi short against the dollar, and silver. Nothing is HIGH conviction with Ueda unstarted and Lagarde at 10:30Z.",

