  geopolitics: "The oil story changed shape overnight, and it changed in a way that resolves an argument this page has been running with itself for three days. For most of the week the dispute here was between two irreconcilable measures of the Strait of Hormuz: IMF PortWatch recording 8 vessel transits on September 13 against an 85-per-day baseline, against US Energy Secretary Wright putting crude transits above 17 million barrels per day versus roughly 20 million before the February 28 attacks. This file first repeated the 90%-down figure, then withdrew it too confidently, then said the honest position was to stop treating either number as settled. The reporting now supplies the reconciliation, and it is mundane rather than political: Saudi Arabia is rerouting crude THROUGH the Strait on shuttle vessels, and ship-to-ship transfers near Oman's Sohar port have risen to 2.7 million barrels per day from 1.5 million in August. Cargo moving on smaller, more numerous or differently counted hulls can produce falling vessel-transit counts and rising crude volumes simultaneously. Both measures can be honest; they are measuring different things. The practical consequence is that crude-flow headlines have lost their power to move the board, which is exactly what the tape shows. WTI has fallen three consecutive sessions to 100.963, -0.93% today, and Brent to 103.602 with its WEEKLY figure NEGATIVE at -0.96%. That decline is happening while the Saudi East-West pipeline is still down - and while the guidance on it has got WORSE, not better: Riyadh now indicates roughly half the damaged capacity returns within days with full operations inside six weeks, against the within-days full restoration Aramco delivered after the April strike. Oil falling on a slower restoration timetable is the market pricing the workaround rather than the repair, and it is the single most important thing on this page today because of where it transmits. It transmits first to Canada, and the transmission is a warning rather than a signal. Yesterday's written trigger said what restores CAD to +1 is the Petroline restart failing and WTI's weekly turning positive. Both have technically happened, and the trigger is being overridden in public: the weekly turned positive only because the seven-day window rolled past the September 10-11 strike spike, while spot fell from 102.173 to 100.963. A terms-of-trade currency does not get upgraded on an arithmetic artefact when its terms of trade are deteriorating, so CAD stays at 0 with services PMI at 46.8 and employment at -41,700 unchanged beneath it. It transmits second to Europe, and it cuts the wrong way for the euro. The gas relief that held EUR at +1 yesterday lasted exactly one session: EU gas is 77.49 and UP 1.49% on the day with its weekly fall shrinking to -2.55% from -5.20%, UK gas 194.56 and UP 2.01% with its week at -1.87% from -6.08%, and only German gas kept falling at -2.15%. Monthlies remain punishing at +22.26% and +24.41% with storage near 68% of capacity, below the five-year average. Meanwhile euro-area energy inflation ran at +14.3% y/y in August, the highest since January 2023, and is now the only thing holding a headline HICP that was revised down to 3.2% - which means the euro area is importing an inflation rate its central bank hiked into, while its domestic gauges cool. That composition, more than any single print, is why EUR was cut to 0. It transmits third to the metals, and here it is constructive. Falling crude pulled inflation expectations and yields lower, stocks and bonds rallied together, and gold rose about 2% to 4367.03 with its weekly turning POSITIVE at +0.38%; silver went further at 66.127 and +2.90% on the week, adding an industrial and physical leg - a sixth consecutive annual supply deficit, lease rates above 5%, Shanghai stocks at their lowest since 2015. Both come off RANGE to LONG. And it transmits to Japan through the external accounts, where the trade balance at -1,106bn yen from -638bn is the Gulf energy bill arriving in the current account; a falling oil price is the one genuine piece of good news the yen has, and it did not stop the currency falling for a seventh session after a 7-2 hike. The standing risk is unchanged and it is not small. Iran is refusing to reopen the Strait without US concessions, a diplomatic effort to establish new shipping rules has been postponed, China has been pressing Iran to curb Houthi attacks on Saudi infrastructure, and reports have Washington weighing resumed strikes ahead of a Gulf leaders meeting. Half a pipeline and a shuttle fleet is a workaround, not a resolution. A second strike on Saudi export infrastructure, or on refining and products, reverses the oil leg, the euro's energy arithmetic, the Canadian neutral, the German equity call and the metals rally inside a single session - which is why nothing on this board is sized as though the war has ended.",

